WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.458 --> 00:00:02.585 align:start size:3% position:97% line:0%
♪

00:00:02.585 --> 00:00:04.129
(Daniel)
We're really excited
about this study that

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:05.922
we just completed
in which we were

00:00:05.922 --> 00:00:07.173
for the first time

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:10.051
able to track single neurons
as they're kind of being placed

00:00:10.051 --> 00:00:10.969
within the brain.

00:00:10.969 --> 00:00:13.221 align:start position:0% line:53%
The field has known
for many years

00:00:13.221 --> 00:00:15.473 align:start position:0% line:53%
that these neurons
are very precisely placed,

00:00:15.807 --> 00:00:18.226 align:start position:0% line:53%
but how that precise placement
happens

00:00:18.226 --> 00:00:19.728
is not really well understood.

00:00:19.728 --> 00:00:21.730
We were able to collaborate
with physicists

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.566
to develop these microscopes
that enabled very gentle

00:00:24.566 --> 00:00:25.900
imaging throughout development

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.986
to be able
to capture those early events.

00:00:28.403 --> 00:00:30.697
And let me show you
how these videos look.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:33.825
We can see early
on the first neurons appearing

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:36.578
and the neurons growing out into
what will be the brain.

00:00:36.786 --> 00:00:39.831
And then we were able to label
single cells and understand

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:42.250
how is the cell
selecting its placement.

00:00:42.792 --> 00:00:43.918
And then it was through

00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:46.963
a process that we called
Retrograde Zippering,

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:49.716
It's like it looks very much
like zippering backwards,

00:00:49.841 --> 00:00:53.553
you know, the way that you close
a jean for example.

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:55.847
Here we're seeing this blue
neuron

00:00:55.847 --> 00:00:58.975
and it's going to zipper
backwards starting at that point

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:03.980
where that green arrow is,
as to meet that other pink

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.065
neuron that is in the correct
neighborhood.

00:01:06.065 --> 00:01:09.694
You can see it actually closing
the gap, that black space

00:01:09.694 --> 00:01:13.031
there as it's now meeting
the correct neighborhood.

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:15.033
This is very important.

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:17.702
What we're able to do with this
microscope is track

00:01:17.702 --> 00:01:21.831
those events as they're actually
occurring and ascribe specific

00:01:22.123 --> 00:01:23.708
genetic functions

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:25.919
to these molecules
that we're identifying

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:27.712
in that sequence of events.

00:01:27.712 --> 00:01:29.339
If your car breaks down,
the reason

00:01:29.339 --> 00:01:31.049
that a mechanic can actually fix
the car

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:31.633
is because

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:33.218
there was an engineer
that built the car

00:01:33.218 --> 00:01:35.512
and they understand
how the car works.

00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:38.681
So over here we have a system
that we didn't build.

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:40.934
We cannot ask the engineer.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.186
So we have to reverse engineer
how it works

00:01:43.186 --> 00:01:45.271
to be able to understand
first how it functions

00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:47.482
and then how it functions
incorrectly.

00:01:47.482 --> 00:01:51.694
Which has implications
to diseases such as autism,

00:01:51.694 --> 00:01:55.031
neurodevelopmental disorders,
mental retardation, etc.

00:01:55.198 --> 00:01:57.075
But it's a fundamental question

00:01:57.075 --> 00:01:59.327
that we need to understand
if we have an aspiration

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:01.663
to be able to understand
how these circuits form

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:05.041
and how they form incorrectly
in early developmental diseases.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:08.211 align:start size:6% position:94% line:0%
♪♪

